## 2. Flutter Development Guidelines

### 2.1 Use `const` Wherever Possible
`const` widgets are built once and reused across rebuilds instead of being
reconstructed every frame — skip it and you're paying a rebuild cost for
something that never changes.

Use `flutter_screenutil` (`.w`, `.h`, `.sp`) and shared `TextStyles` for
responsive spacing and typography — those values are runtime-sized and cannot be
`const`. Mark the widget itself `const` and reserve `const` for subtrees whose
values truly never change (icons, static labels, fixed durations).

```dart
// ❌ BAD: fixed pixel values, no const constructor, rebuilt every parent rebuild
class HeaderBanner extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Container(
      padding: EdgeInsets.all(16),
      child: Text('Welcome', style: TextStyle(fontSize: 20)),
    );
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD: responsive sizing via ScreenUtil, shared TextStyles, const constructor
class HeaderBanner extends StatelessWidget {
  const HeaderBanner({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Padding(
      padding: EdgeInsets.all(16.w),
      child: Text('Welcome', style: TextStyles.medium20()),
    );
  }
}
```

Enable `prefer_const_constructors` and `prefer_const_literals_to_create_immutables`
in `analysis_options.yaml` so the analyzer catches missed opportunities instead of
relying on manual review.

### 2.2 Proper Widget Composition
Break large `build()` methods into small, named widgets instead of deeply nested
anonymous trees. Composition — not inheritance, not giant methods — is how Flutter
UIs stay maintainable.

```dart
// ❌ BAD: one build() method doing everything, 5+ levels of nesting, fixed sizes
class ProfileScreen extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      body: Column(
        children: [
          Container(
            padding: EdgeInsets.all(16),
            child: Row(
              children: [
                CircleAvatar(backgroundImage: NetworkImage(user.avatarUrl)),
                Column(
                  crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.start,
                  children: [
                    Text(user.name, style: TextStyle(fontSize: 18)),
                    Text(user.email, style: TextStyle(color: Colors.grey)),
                  ],
                ),
              ],
            ),
          ),
          // ...another 100 lines
        ],
      ),
    );
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD: each piece is a small, independently testable widget with responsive sizing
class ProfileScreen extends StatelessWidget {
  const ProfileScreen({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return const Scaffold(
      body: Column(
        children: [_ProfileHeader()],
      ),
    );
  }
}

class _ProfileHeader extends StatelessWidget {
  const _ProfileHeader();

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Padding(
      padding: EdgeInsets.all(16.w),
      child: Row(
        children: const [_ProfileAvatar(), _ProfileNameAndEmail()],
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

A widget class is the right extraction unit when a chunk of the tree (a) has a
clear name, (b) could plausibly be reused, or (c) is long enough to obscure the
surrounding structure.

### 2.3 Null Safety — Handle, Don't Silence
The `!` operator turns a compile-time null check into a runtime crash waiting to
happen. Prefer patterns that make the null case explicit and handled.

```dart
// ❌ BAD: force-unwrapping hides the null case until it crashes in production
String getDisplayName(User? user) {
  return user!.name!;
}

// ✅ GOOD: the null case is a decision, not a crash
String getDisplayName(User? user) {
  return user?.name ?? 'Guest';
}

// ✅ GOOD: guard clause when null truly is an error case
String getDisplayName(User? user) {
  if (user == null) throw ArgumentError('User must not be null here');
  return user.name;
}
```

- Reach for `!` only when nullability was already narrowed just above (e.g. right
  after an `if (x != null)` check the analyzer can't yet promote automatically).
- Prefer `late` over `!`-laden nullable fields only when the field is *guaranteed*
  to be set before first read (e.g. initialized in `initState`) — otherwise it just
  moves the crash from "wrong value" to "not yet assigned."
- Model "value or absent" with `?`/`??`, not with sentinel values like `-1` or `''`.

### 2.4 Async/Await Best Practices
Async code has Flutter-specific failure modes beyond plain Dart: widgets can be
disposed mid-`await`, and `BuildContext` can go stale. Keep async work in Cubits
and Blocs — widgets should dispatch events, not own `await`/`setState` cycles.

```dart
// ❌ BAD: async logic in the widget — errors swallowed, setState after dispose
class _SearchFieldState extends State<SearchField> {
  Future<void> _onSubmit(String query) async {
    final results = await _api.search(query); // if this throws, no one catches it
    setState(() => _results = results); // may run after the widget is disposed
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD: Cubit owns the async work; widget only dispatches
class SearchCubit extends Cubit<SearchState> {
  SearchCubit(this._api) : super(const SearchInitial());

  final SearchApi _api;

  Future<void> search(String query) async {
    emit(const SearchLoading());
    try {
      final results = await _api.search(query);
      emit(SearchSuccess(results));
    } on ApiException catch (e) {
      emit(SearchError(e.message));
    }
  }
}

// In the widget — no await, no setState, no mounted check needed
onSubmitted: (query) => context.read<SearchCubit>().search(query),
```

- Only event handlers (`onPressed`, etc.) should be `async void`; everything else
  returns `Future<T>` so callers can `await` and handle errors.
- When a widget must `await` (e.g. `showDialog`), check `context.mounted` before
  using `context` or calling `Navigator` after the `await`.
- Never fire-and-forget a `Future` that can fail — at minimum attach
  `.catchError(...)` or await it.
- Use `Future.wait` for independent async calls instead of awaiting them
  sequentially when there's no dependency between them.

### 2.5 Error Handling
Every async boundary (network, disk, platform channel) needs an explicit failure
path — the user should see something actionable, not a red screen or a silent no-op.
Model loading, success, and failure as distinct Cubit/Bloc states.

```dart
// ❌ BAD: no failure path — a network error just leaves the UI stuck loading
class OrdersCubit extends Cubit<OrdersState> {
  OrdersCubit(this._repository) : super(const OrdersInitial());

  final OrdersRepository _repository;

  Future<void> loadOrders() async {
    final orders = await _repository.fetchOrders();
    emit(OrdersLoaded(orders));
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD: failure has a distinct, user-facing state
class OrdersCubit extends Cubit<OrdersState> {
  OrdersCubit(this._repository) : super(const OrdersInitial());

  final OrdersRepository _repository;

  Future<void> loadOrders() async {
    emit(const OrdersLoading());
    try {
      final orders = await _repository.fetchOrders();
      emit(OrdersLoaded(orders));
    } on NetworkException catch (_) {
      emit(const OrdersOffline());
    } on ApiException catch (e) {
      emit(OrdersError(e.message));
    }
  }
}
```

- Catch specific exception types (see clean-code §1.8) rather than bare `catch (e)`
  — a bare catch can mask bugs (e.g. a `NoSuchMethodError`) as if they were expected
  failures.
- Never leave an empty `catch` block. If an error genuinely can be ignored, say so
  with a comment explaining why.
- For Firebase/API/payment integrations, map raw SDK exceptions to your own
  domain exceptions at the boundary, so the rest of the app never depends on a
  third-party SDK's exception shape directly.

### 2.6 Lifecycle & Disposal
Anything a widget creates that outlives a single `build()` call —
`AnimationController`, `TextEditingController`, `FocusNode`, `StreamSubscription`,
`ScrollController` — must be released in `dispose()`, or it leaks and can keep
firing callbacks into a dead widget.

```dart
// ❌ BAD: controller and subscription are never cleaned up
class _VideoPlayerState extends State<VideoPlayer> with SingleTickerProviderStateMixin {
  late final AnimationController _controller =
      AnimationController(vsync: this, duration: const Duration(seconds: 1));
  late final StreamSubscription<Position> _positionSub =
      widget.player.positionStream.listen(_onPositionChanged);

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => /* ... */ const SizedBox();
}

// ✅ GOOD: everything acquired is released
class _VideoPlayerState extends State<VideoPlayer> with SingleTickerProviderStateMixin {
  late final AnimationController _controller =
      AnimationController(vsync: this, duration: const Duration(seconds: 1));
  late final StreamSubscription<Position> _positionSub =
      widget.player.positionStream.listen(_onPositionChanged);

  @override
  void dispose() {
    _controller.dispose();
    _positionSub.cancel();
    super.dispose();
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => /* ... */ const SizedBox();
}
```

- Pair every `Controller()`/`.listen()`/`FocusNode()` with a matching disposal call
  in the same review pass — treat them as acquire/release pairs.
- Always call `super.dispose()` **last**, after your own cleanup.
- In Cubits, cancel subscriptions and close streams in `close()` — call
  `super.close()` last, same discipline as `dispose()`.

### 2.7 Avoid Unnecessary Rebuilds
A rebuild that doesn't change anything on screen is wasted work — and at scale
(long lists, animations) it's the difference between smooth and janky.

```dart
// ❌ BAD: BlocBuilder rebuilds on ANY state change, even fields this widget ignores
class CartTotalLabel extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return BlocBuilder<CartCubit, CartState>(
      builder: (context, state) {
        if (state is! CartLoaded) return const SizedBox.shrink();
        return Text('Total: \$${state.total}');
      },
    );
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD: BlocSelector narrows rebuilds to the one field this widget displays
class CartTotalLabel extends StatelessWidget {
  const CartTotalLabel({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return BlocSelector<CartCubit, CartState, double>(
      selector: (state) => state is CartLoaded ? state.total : 0,
      builder: (context, total) => Text('Total: \$$total'),
    );
  }
}
```

- Extract the parts of a widget tree that change into their own small widgets, so
  a rebuild higher up doesn't force unrelated `const` subtrees to rebuild.
- In Bloc/Cubit, use `BlocSelector` or `BlocBuilder` with `buildWhen` to watch
  only the specific field a widget reads — not the whole state object.
- Avoid defining closures (`onTap: () => doThing()`) inline inside frequently
  rebuilt widgets when the callback can be hoisted to a stable method reference —
  it avoids unnecessary `didUpdateWidget` diffing churn in some cases, though this
  matters far less than the `const`/`BlocSelector` points above.
- Use `ListView.builder`/`GridView.builder` (lazy) instead of building a full
  `List<Widget>` up front for long or unbounded lists.
- Add `Key`s to list items when the list can reorder, insert, or remove — without
  one, Flutter can't tell which widget maps to which data and may rebuild or
  animate the wrong element.

